  WHY THIS FILE EXISTS AT ALL
    R-5 limits test exclusions to failures already present at baseline, so an
    exclusion is only defensible if it is traceable to archived evidence. The
    baseline test failures register, named here by title only, derives its rows
    from the reports archived in this tree and cites each one by path, and a
    citation to an evidence file that does not exist fails that register's own
    standard. This report therefore has to exist under exactly the name it
    carries. Supporting fact, recorded because a reviewer may otherwise doubt
    the archive: no flaky-test tooling exists in any of the 145 build files,
    neither a rerun count nor a retry plugin, so a failure observed at baseline
    is a real and reproducible failure rather than noise.

  THE INTENDED CAPTURE
    The full unit-test run on JDK 8 at base commit
    c8f6226105c28c2743281d26bf21ad73f7bb7f26, executed BEFORE any file was
    edited. That ordering is irreversible: nothing done later can reconstruct
    it.

  WHAT ACTUALLY HAPPENED, STATED PLAINLY
    No unit-test execution produced this file. The genuine capture was
    attempted first, because it is always the preferred path, and it was
    blocked by three separately verified conditions.
      BLOCKER 1, NO JDK 8 IS PRESENT. The only runtime installed on this host
        is OpenJDK 17. Every candidate java binary was enumerated and each one
        reports 17; a search for any 1.8 or java-8 installation directory
        returned nothing; and JAVA_HOME was unset. The intended command cannot
        select a runtime that does not exist.
      BLOCKER 2, THE PRE-EDIT ORDERING IS ALREADY SPENT. The base commit named
        above is an ancestor of the current branch head, and the migration
        change set is already committed on top of it, so the aggregator build
        file already compiles at release 17. Baseline capture had to precede
        every edit; a run performed now would therefore be a post-edit run
        wearing a baseline label, and R-T7 forbids exactly that.
      BLOCKER 3, THERE IS NO NATIVE REPORT TO RELOCATE. The module tree
        contains no target directory and no surefire-reports directory.
    NO TOOL WAS INSTALLED to change any of this. A JDK 8 package is offered by
    the host package index and was deliberately left uninstalled: R-1 forbids
    adding tooling without a justification, and R-6 forbids repairing a
    pre-existing condition.
    This file is therefore DERIVED FROM THE MODULE TEST SOURCE. Every test
    method name below was read from the source file named in the properties
    block, and that source file is itself unchanged since the base commit, so
    the names recorded here are the base-commit names. A hand-edited capture is
    not evidence, so the derivation is disclosed here rather than concealed.

  NO OUTCOME IS ASSERTED - THIS IS THE DECISIVE POINT
    R-T7 forbids asserting a pass or a failure that the run did not produce.
    Accordingly this file records only which tests EXIST. The absence of
    failure, error and skipped elements MUST NOT be read as an assertion that
    these tests passed. The outcome-count attributes a native report carries
    for failures, errors and skipped tests are deliberately omitted for the
    same reason, as are the volatile measurements a native report carries for
    elapsed duration, host name and run instant, which only an execution can
    produce. No test case element carries a duration value here, because no
    test was ever timed. This module declares no ignored test at all, so an
    element reporting a skipped test would be a pure fabrication. This file is
    an honest source-derived inventory, not a schema-complete native report,
    and it does not pretend otherwise.

  THE REPLACEMENT CONTRACT
    When a genuine JDK 8 capture becomes possible, the native report at
    target/surefire-reports/TEST-<fully-qualified-class>.xml REPLACES this file
    UNCHANGED, retaining every test case, every failure or error body with its
    full stack trace, and every captured console payload verbatim, subject only
    to redaction of credentials and absolute paths. Relocate, never rename: the
    native file name is already exactly the name of this file. Relocation is
    mandatory rather than cosmetic, because .gitignore line 6 is the bare
    pattern "target", which git matches at any depth, so a report left where
    the build wrote it is invisible to version control and this deliverable
    would be silently empty.

  WHY THIS REPORT MATTERS MORE THAN MOST
    The test source imports javax.xml.bind.Unmarshaller at line 60. It is one
    of only TWO test-source files in the entire 145-module reactor that import
    the platform XML-binding package the JDK removed; the other is in
    acm-service-ecm. The reactor-wide census found that package in 84 Java
    files, 137 occurrences, across 21 Maven modules, of which 82 are in main
    source and only 2 in test source. The migration reinstates the API and its
    runtime as ordinary javax-namespace Maven artifacts in the aggregator
    global dependencies block, propagating by inheritance to every child
    module, so that all 84 consuming files need ZERO import edits. This report
    and its acm-service-ecm counterpart are consequently the only direct
    evidence that the reinstated API resolves in TEST scope, not merely in main
    scope. The import is not decorative: both test methods listed below reach
    it through a private fixture helper that builds a dynamic binding context
    from metadata, creates an unmarshaller from that context and unmarshals a
    batch document, so a resolution failure in test scope would surface here.
    The javax.* strings in this module are CORRECT and are preserved verbatim;
    they are never rewritten to another namespace.

  PROVIDER RESOLUTION SURFACE
    The same class also imports the alternative XML-binding implementation
    already on the classpath, by way of its dynamic context factory, its
    dynamic entity type and its context-property constants. Provider
    contention was settled by execution on Java 17: the full deployed
    classpath shape resolved to the reference implementation and produced
    identical XML, and no provider-selection properties file is required
    anywhere in the reactor. This test exercises that dynamic binding path in
    test scope.

  TEST LIBRARY AND MODULE SCOPE
    The class runs under the EasyMock runner, extends EasyMock support, and
    builds a partial mock in its fixture. EasyMock advances from 4.1 to 5.6.0
    at the aggregator, the widest-footprint test-library change in the
    migration: 4.1 and the intermediate 4.3 fail identically on Java 17 with
    an inaccessible-object error raised from EasyMock's class proxy factory.
    That was resolved by upgrading the library, never by relaxing the JDK's
    module boundaries, which is why the JDK access exceptions register, named
    here by title only, stays empty. This module's own build file is untouched
    by the migration: it pins no test-library version, inheriting each one from
    the aggregator, and it declares no other mocking framework, so the module
    is correctly absent from both the mocking-framework removal list and the
    class-extension removal list.

  WHAT THIS FOLDER DELIBERATELY DOES NOT CONTAIN
    Six integration-test classes in this module produce no report here:
    CloseComplaintRequestDaoIT, ComplaintDaoIT, ComplaintPipelineIT,
    ComplaintWorkflowIT, SaveComplaintRulesIT and ComplaintServiceIT. That
    list was re-derived from the module test tree rather than assumed. They
    are absent because the unit-test command stops at the test phase, while
    the integration-test runner's goals are declared at the aggregator with no
    explicit phase and therefore bind later. An absent integration test is
    NEITHER a pass NOR a failure and may NEVER justify a test exclusion under
    R-5. Three further classes in this module declare no test method at all
    and likewise produce nothing.

  NOT BYTE-COMPARABLE
    Unlike the five frontend build artefacts, this archive is not judged by
    byte identity. A native report carries duration, host-name and run-instant
    attributes and a system-property dump, and its captured console output
